# Global Coding-Agent Policy: Conventional Commits + Logical Grouping

These instructions apply to all git-using work unless the user explicitly says otherwise.

## Core rule

When committing changes:
1. Use Conventional Commits format for every commit message.
2. Group changes into commits by logical unit of work — one coherent change per commit — so history is easy to scan, review, revert, and bisect.

## Conventional Commits format

```
<type>(<optional scope>): <short imperative summary>

<optional body explaining the why, not the what>

<optional footer(s): BREAKING CHANGE:, Refs #123, Co-Authored-By:, etc.>
```

**Allowed types:**
- `feat` — a new user-visible feature
- `fix` — a bug fix
- `docs` — documentation only
- `style` — formatting, whitespace, no behavior change
- `refactor` — internal restructure, no behavior change
- `perf` — performance improvement
- `test` — adding or fixing tests only
- `build` — build system, dependencies, packaging
- `ci` — CI configuration
- `chore` — maintenance that doesn't fit elsewhere
- `revert` — reverts a previous commit

**Rules:**
- Subject line: imperative mood ("add", not "added"/"adds"), no trailing period, ≤72 chars.
- Scope is optional but encouraged when one area of the codebase is touched (e.g., `feat(auth):`, `fix(api):`).
- Breaking changes: append `!` after type/scope (`feat(api)!:`) AND include a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer explaining the break.
- Body wraps at ~72 chars, explains motivation and contrast with previous behavior.

## Logical grouping rules

Before committing, look at the working tree and split changes into the smallest set of coherent commits:

- **One concern per commit.** A bug fix and an unrelated refactor are two commits, not one.
- **Tests with the change they prove.** New behavior + its tests go together (the TDD pair). Fixing a flaky test unrelated to current work is its own commit.
- **Refactors before features when possible.** If a refactor enables the feature, commit the refactor first (it should be behavior-preserving and green on its own), then the feature on top.
- **Mechanical changes separate from semantic changes.** Renames, formatting sweeps, and dependency bumps should not be mixed into a feature commit — they bury the real diff.
- **Each commit should build and pass tests on its own** when reasonably achievable, so `git bisect` works.
- **Don't bundle unrelated fixes** just because they were noticed in the same session.

If the working tree has mixed changes, use `git add -p` (or stage specific files/hunks) to split them — don't `git add -A` and hope for the best.

## Required workflow

When asked to commit:
1. Run `git status` and `git diff` to see everything that changed.
2. Identify logical groups; state the planned commit sequence to the user before staging if there is more than one group.
3. Stage each group explicitly (specific files or hunks).
4. Write the commit message in Conventional Commits format.
5. Repeat for each group.
6. Run `git log --oneline` at the end so the user can see the resulting history.

## Examples

Good:
- `feat(auth): add password reset endpoint`
- `fix(parser): handle empty input without panicking`
- `refactor(db): extract connection pool into its own module`
- `test(auth): cover expired-token edge case`
- `feat(api)!: drop deprecated /v1/users endpoint`

Bad:
- `update stuff`
- `fix bug and add feature and clean up imports` (three commits, not one)
- `WIP` (not a final commit message)
- `Fixed login.` (past tense, trailing period, no type)

## Never do this

- Do not squash unrelated work into one commit to "save time."
- Do not write commit messages that describe the diff instead of the intent.
- Do not use `git add -A` / `git add .` when the working tree has mixed concerns — stage selectively.
- Do not amend or force-push commits the user hasn't approved that action for.

